      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "# Kernels with no teacher\n\nEvery other example in this pack shows the network the right answer. This one has no right\nanswer anywhere in it — no labels, no target image, nothing to copy. It has **one\nphotograph** and a statement about what a good filter *does*.\n\nWhat comes out are oriented edge detectors, at a range of angles and sizes. The same small\nalphabet that falls out of sparse coding (Olshausen & Field, 1996), out of ICA on natural\nscenes (Bell & Sejnowski, 1997), out of the first layer of essentially any convolutional\nnet trained on photographs — and much the same as what has been measured in mammalian\nprimary visual cortex.\n\nNobody wrote the word \"edge\" anywhere in this graph. **Edges are simply what is left** when\nyou ask for a filter whose response to a photograph is *rarely* large.\n\nLoad your own picture into the LoadImage node. The alphabet changes with it: a face gives\nyou different letters than a brick wall, and a screenshot of text gives you strokes."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        -40,
        620
      ],
      "size": [
        620,
        660
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 1,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "## Why not just \"make the histogram change a lot\"?\n\nThat is the obvious idea, and it is worth running once. Set **objective** to\n`histogram change` and press run. You get sixteen tiles of static, and the report says so:\n\n```\nsparsity   0.619   (untrained: 0.621)\nloudness   0.039\ndistinctness 0.904\n```\n\nUntrained kernels score **0.621** on this picture. Trained ones score **0.619**. Nothing was\nlearned. Two separate things are wrong, and they are worth separating.\n\n**It is not blind to scale.** \"How different is the histogram\" changes if you multiply the\nresponse by a constant — so the optimiser optimises the constant and leaves the filter\nalone. On mean-centred patches the cheapest histogram maximally unlike a photograph's is a\nsingle spike at zero, so the bank **switches itself off**: that `loudness 0.039` is a\nresponse 26 times *smaller* than the input. A filter bank whose best available move is to\noutput nothing at all.\n\nNow turn `diversity` up to 1 with `histogram change` still selected. It bolts the other way\n— **75x** the size of the input — and still learns nothing (0.623 against the same 0.621).\nEither way, the thing being trained is the volume knob.\n\n`sparse response` is a **ratio**, E|r| over the rms, so multiplying the response by\nanything at all leaves it exactly where it was. Gain earns nothing. The only way left to\nmove the score is to change the *shape* of the distribution — which was the interesting\npart all along."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        620,
        620
      ],
      "size": [
        600,
        660
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 2,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "## Why `diversity` is not optional\n\nSet it to **0**, leave the objective on `sparse response`, and run again:\n\n```\nSparsity improved 0.077 on the\nuntrained floor — each kernel is quiet\nover most of the image and loud in a\nfew places...\nThe kernels have collapsed: on average\neach one is 87% the same as its\nnearest neighbour.\n```\n\nRead those two together, then look at the run you just did with diversity at 1.0: it gained\n**0.069**. The collapsed bank scores **better**. It is not a near miss or a rounding error\n— by the only measure being optimised, throwing away fifteen of your sixteen filters is an\nimprovement.\n\nOf course it is. Each kernel is scored **on its own**, so sixteen copies of the best answer\nare sixteen good answers as far as the objective can see. No better statistic fixes this,\nand **no amount of staring at the loss curve reveals it**, because the curve is genuinely\nlower in the collapsed run. The only thing that catches it is a measurement the objective\ndoes not make: how alike the kernels are.\n\n`diversity` adds the missing sentence — penalise any two kernels for responding to the same\npatches. 1.0 is a good default. Above about 3 they start to smear; at 10 they are worse on\nevery measure at once."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        1240,
        620
      ],
      "size": [
        620,
        660
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 3,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "## What you are looking at\n\n**Filter Bank** is the one to look at first — every learned kernel slid across your actual\npicture, at full resolution. Sixteen readings of the same photograph, one per letter of the\nalphabet the model just invented. A bank learned from 12x12 patches runs unchanged over a\nmegapixel image, because that is what a convolution *is*: the same small operation applied\neverywhere, with no size baked in.\n\nSet its `layout` to **batch** and you get sixteen separate ComfyUI IMAGEs instead of one\ncontact sheet — feed them to an upscaler, a ControlNet, Video Combine, or img2img, exactly\nlike any other image in ComfyUI. Nothing downstream needs to know they came from a filter\nthat trained itself thirty seconds ago.\n\n**View Weights** — the kernels themselves, magnified. Light and dark lobes side by side at\nsome angle: that is an edge detector, and nobody put it there.\n\n**Response Histogram** — the distribution the objective is actually optimising, drawn\nagainst the same filters before training. Both curves are scaled to the same width first,\nbecause otherwise this would be a picture of gain. What is left is the shape: a taller\nspike at zero and fatter tails. Silent nearly everywhere, emphatic in a few places.\n\nOne number to distrust: the raw sparsity score. Photographs are already strongly\nnon-Gaussian before any filter touches them, so the report always quotes what **untrained**\nkernels score on *this* image. Random kernels score 0.798 on Gaussian noise exactly as\ntheory says, 0.62 on this beach, and **0.52 on a screenshot of text** — better than a\nwell-trained bank manages on the beach. Only the gap from the floor means anything."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
